  "notes": "{閉|と}じる means to close, typically used for eyes, books, and metaphorical closings.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Can be transitive or intransitive\n- Transitive: {目|め}を{閉|と}じる (to close one's eyes)\n- Intransitive: {幕|まく}が{閉|と}じる (the curtain closes)\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {目|め}を{閉|と}じる: to close one's eyes\n- {本|ほん}を{閉|と}じる: to close a book\n- {口|くち}を{閉|と}じる: to shut one's mouth\n- {幕|まく}を{閉|と}じる: to bring to a close (figurative)\n- {生涯|しょうがい}を{閉|と}じる: to end one's life\n\nDISTINCTION (homophones):\n- {閉|と}じる: to close (eyes, books, figurative)\n- {綴|と}じる: to bind, staple (papers)\n\nCONTRAST WITH {閉|し}める:\n{閉|と}じる is for things that open like a cover or eyelids; {閉|し}める is for doors, windows, and containers.",
